  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Pending Stages**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `OperatorDashboard.tsx` file, specifically lines 223-240. I\'m focusing on the conditional rendering for accepting or rejecting a booking based on its \'pending\' stage. I\'m thinking through how this impacts the user flow and potential edge cases.\n\n\n**Refining Acceptance Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the implications of accepting or rejecting bookings, particularly concerning the user interface. I\'m considering Requirement 4, which involves dynamic tracking, including showing the pickup location and ambulance search status initially. I\'m focusing on how the UI transitions after acceptance, displaying the assigned ambulance and its route. The evolution of the route, and vehicle position during the trip, is also under scrutiny.\n\n\n**Integrating Map Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating the map into the booking and tracking interface immediately following submission. I\'m focusing on Requirement 3: centering the map on the user\'s actual GPS location or entered pickup point. I\'m considering the UI to show the pickup marker, destination marker, assigned ambulance, and route if available. I\'ve also incorporated dynamic tracking per Requirement 4: initially displaying the ambulance search status and then showing the assigned ambulance and updated route post-acceptance.\n\n\n**Prioritizing UI Cleanliness**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI/UX, starting with homepage design. I\'ve decided to eliminate the map from the homepage to keep it clean and professional, focusing on booking initiation. I\'ll include the 5-second hold button, the booking form, emergency contacts, and recent trip history. Requirement 2 is key: the map will appear *only* after a booking is initiated, and will open in the booking/tracking interface after a successful 5-second hold or form submission.\n\n\n**Enhancing Map Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the interactive Leaflet map into the user interface! It will be prominently displayed in the booking/tracking view, whether the operator or user is tracking the trip. If a user cancels the booking or the trip completes, they will return to the map-free homepage. Requirement 3 is key: the map must accurately center on the user\'s GPS pickup location, if permission is granted, or their manually selected pickup point. I will display the pickup and destination markers, ambulance, and the route.\n\n\n**Defining Pending States**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the states for a new booking: initiating a booking, I\'ve defined a \'pending\' state. I\'ll show the pickup and destination markers. The status will indicate the search for the nearest ambulance. Until the operator accepts, the map won\'t display the ambulance or the route. I\'m simulating operator dispatch with auto-acceptance or allowing manual acceptance.\n\n\n**Mapping Booking Progress**\n\nI\'m now focusing on what happens when the operator accepts the booking. The map will transition to show the road-following route from ambulance to pickup. The ambulance marker animates on the road toward the pickup, and the live ETA and distance will update. As the trip progresses, the route and vehicle position will update at each stage. I\'ve also verified that the map interface remains consistent regardless of whether the booking is made through the 5-second hold or the manual form. I\'m also ensuring booking details are preserved, and that duplicate requests are prevented.\n\n\n"
