  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Active Booking Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the display logic for active bookings within `UserDashboard.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m examining lines 180-294. My focus is on the conditional rendering of the active booking live card when `activeBooking` exists and is in a stage other than \'completed\' or \'cancelled\'. I\'m also watching out for the `Leaf`.\n\n\n**Refining Map Display Logic**\n\nI\'ve just realized a critical issue: the `LeafletMap` component was persistently rendered in `App.tsx`, making it always visible, which violates user requirements. I\'m now tasked with ensuring it only appears after booking initiation. The goal is a cleaner homepage and an interactive map only after a successful booking. The map should be centered on the user\'s location.\n\n\n**Defining Map Behavior**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the map\'s role. It will dynamically show the pickup point and ambulance-search status initially, updating to display the assigned ambulance and route once an operator accepts. I must ensure the map interface is consistent across all booking methods. It\'s critical that the map is never on the homepage, only appearing during an active trip. I also need to make sure we\'re handling things like GPS permission and routing failures gracefully.\n\n\n**Designing Emergency Dispatch**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the emergency dispatch interface. The user will be greeted with several options: the \"User\" or \"Operator\" view, a 3D telemetry viewer, and a \"Reset Demo\" button. The sub-navigation tabs will include \"Emergency Dispatch,\" \"Hospital Directory,\" \"Trip History,\" and \"Patient Medical ID.\" The \"Emergency Dispatch\" hero will feature a GPS status indicator, a large \"Hold to Confirm\" button for instant booking, and a \"Manual Booking\" toggle. There\'s no map on this screen.\n\n\n**Clarifying Booking Lifecycle**\n\nI\'m now detailing the lifecycle of a new active booking. When created, its stage is \'pending\' and the `trackingCode` is generated. Key data points, like pickup and dropoff coordinates and addresses, are also stored. Critically, initially, `assignedUnitId` is undefined and `routePolyline` is empty. The initial status will be \"Searching for nearest\".\n\n\n**Visualizing Trip Tracking**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the transition to the Active Booking/Tracking View. The interactive Leaflet map now *is* displayed, centered on the user\'s pickup. I can see the pickup marker, potentially with a radar ring, and the destination hospital marker. While pending, telemetry will show the ambulance search, triage transmission, and operator acceptance wait. The operator interface is also working, with an \"Accept Emergency Request\" button.\n\n\n**Mapping Trip Progression**\n\nI\'m now detailing the behavior after operator acceptance of a new booking. The stage advances to \'accepted\' or \'en_route_pickup\', and an ambulance (like \'amb-aiims-1\') is assigned. The route from the ambulance\'s current location to the pickup point is calculated and drawn on the map, with a siren ring, driver details, and ambulance marker appearing. As the trip progresses, the vehicle position and route polyline dynamically update until completion or cancellation, returning the user to a map-free homepage. I\'m also now considering the need for map display in the Operator view.\n\n\n**Determining Map\'s Proper Location**\n\nI\'m now determining the correct place to render the map, either in `App.tsx` or directly within the active booking tracking interface. I have defined the necessary conditions for display: the map will appear in the \"operator\" view or within the \"user\" view when there\'s an active booking, and the booking stage is not \'completed\' or \'cancelled\'. The goal is to eliminate the map\'s homepage presence, instead showing it only after booking. I am leaning toward embedding the map component inside the Active Booking interface.\n\n\n**Visualizing Homepage Transition**\n\nNow, I\'m envisioning the complete homepage transformation. With no active booking, the interface is clean: just the header, tabs, and Emergency Dispatch Hero with its components. No map is present. The booking triggers display of the active booking interface instantly. This brings the interactive Leaflet Map to the top, centered on the user\'s pickup with markers and a dynamic dispatch status.\n\n\n**Integrating Operator View**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating the operator\'s perspective. After operator acceptance, the map will display the assigned ambulance, route, live ETA, and distance. I\'m also adding a telemetry strip, driver contact card with a call button, 3D telemetry and share trip link, and a cancellation button. Crucially, I\'ve realized the operator view in `OperatorDashboard.tsx` also needs the map, enabling visualization of incoming requests and unit assignments.\n\n\n**Refining Home Visibility**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the map\'s conditional rendering. It seems the best solution is to wrap the `LeafletMap` component within a conditional block in `App.tsx`, controlled by the `currentView` state. The map will only render when the view is \'user\' and there\'s an active booking that\'s not \'completed\' or \'cancelled\', as well as the \'operator\' view. The homepage will remain clean, with the map appearing dynamically after booking submission, centered on the pickup point.\n\n\n**Designing Map Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the map into the active booking interface. Requirement 2 clearly specifies its immediate appearance after booking submission, so I\'m rendering it within the tracking view in `UserDashboard`. I\'m verifying both booking methods utilize the same map, preserving details and handling potential errors. The design guarantees map accessibility throughout the active trip, and never on the homepage.\n\n\n**Visualizing Active Booking Interface**\n\nI am now focusing on the user-facing active booking view, specifically the elements following booking submission. This incorporates the active booking header which includes the tracking code, stage badge, and interactive leaflet map. The map\'s appearance is crucial, centered on the user\'s pickup with distinct markers for the pickup and destination. I\'ve considered how it displays ambulance search status and ambulance details after acceptance. I\'m focusing on the responsive design and interactive elements.\n\n\n\n**Visualizing Trip Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing the full trip progression after ambulance assignment. The map will dynamically show a road-following polyline from the ambulance\'s location to the pickup point. The assigned ambulance is marked with a siren pulse and heading. The map also contains a real-time telemetry strip that is comprised of live ETA, Road distance, the dispatched unit and the destination hospital. Furthermore, the details of the assigned driver and a direct contact card is also present. The trip stage progression stepper clearly moves from Pending to Completed. The Patient & Medical details card has the pickup address with gps accuracy, destination address and reported symptoms/notes. Also, there is no map rendered on the homepage when `activeBooking === null`.\n\n\n**Defining Homepage Structure**\n\nI\'m now detailing the homepage layout. The homepage presents branding, a view switcher, a 3D viewer trigger, and a reset demo button within the header. Below, there are several sub-navigation tabs, including \"Emergency Dispatch,\" \"Hospital Directory,\" \"Trip History,\" and \"Patient Medical ID.\" The primary tab is \"Emergency Dispatch,\" featuring a hero with a GPS pickup status, a \"Hold to Confirm\" button, and a \"Manual Booking\" toggle.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Homepage Map Absence**\n\nI\'m now verifying the map\'s absence on the homepage. I\'m confirming that the map *never* renders on any homepage tab. Specifically, the map only appears during an active booking. If a booking is cancelled or completed, the map disappears, and the user is redirected to the clean homepage. I\'m also ensuring the \"Reset Demo\" button properly resets the application state, removing the map from the display.\n\n\n**Validating Initial State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the app behaves during initial load, and upon resets. I am verifying that the initial booking state is correctly handled, especially concerning the `INITIAL_DEMO_BOOKING`. If the user is a first-timer, or the local storage is corrupted, the application should default to a clean homepage, ensuring no map appears unless a booking is active. This also includes handling legacy demo bookings in `localStorage`.\n\n\n**Refining Initial Homepage**\n\nI\'m now refining the initial homepage state, especially after considering potential local storage issues. I\'m verifying that if only the demo booking exists in `localStorage`, the application resets to a clean homepage, removing any pre-loaded map. I have added code to address this scenario, ensuring a pristine user experience on first visit, or after a demo reset, with the map absent until a new booking.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Initial Homepage**\n\nI can now confirm that the application correctly displays a clean homepage upon initial load or after a reset. The crucial element is verifying no map renders initially. Then, I\'m examining how we handle the GPS pickup location for accurate map centering. I am now double-checking the process that ensures we are always requesting and refreshing the user\'s actual geolocation, which is pivotal for accurately displaying the pickup and route, as required.\n\n\n\n**Validating Location Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now implementing and validating the logic for accurate map centering and marker placement. I am setting the pickup location using `setPickup({ coords, address })`, which handles GPS data, gracefully failing to the manual location. I\'m ensuring the map centers correctly on `pickup.coords`, whether the user\'s actual GPS or a manually selected landmark. I am also making sure the pickup marker with the GPS accuracy circle and destination marker are displayed accordingly.\n\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Progression**\n\nI\'m now tracing the step-by-step progression of the booking lifecycle, focusing on the transition to the Tracking View. I\'ve broken down each state and data point to confirm the proper state updates and data persistence. From initial booking request submission, including the generation of the `trackingCode` and the \"Searching for nearest\" status. The pickup address, destination address, and GPS coordinates are also set accordingly.\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Progression**\n\nI am now tracing the progression after booking submission through operator acceptance. Specifically, I\'m confirming the tracking view displays during the `\'pending\'` stage, showing the Leaflet map centered on pickup coordinates. The map shows the pickup marker (with radar effect), destination marker, and the searching status badge and message. I\'ve also verified the Operator view shows the incoming request with the \"Accept Emergency Request\" button. I\'ve included a user-friendly \"Simulate Operator Acceptance\" button for easy testing.\n\n\n\n**Mapping Route Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now detailing the system\'s reaction when an operator accepts a request. The logic selects the nearest ambulance and then calculates the route, setting crucial trip details like the assigned unit ID, the route polyline, and the current vehicle position. I\'m focusing on the map\'s instantaneous update, which visualizes the ambulance\'s new position and the planned route.\n\n\n\n**Visualizing Trip Completion**\n\nI am now focusing on the complete trip cycle and map display dynamics. The map dynamically updates with the live ETA, distance, driver details, and ambulance information, like the photo, call sign and speed. As the ambulance approaches the pickup, the stage transitions to \'on_scene\'. After transportation starts, the route updates to the destination hospital. On arrival, the stage is set to \'completed\' with the map removed, and the user returns to a homepage with no map.\n\n\n**Defining Map Rendering Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the logic controlling map display in both `App.tsx` and `OperatorDashboard.tsx`. After careful review, I\'ve concluded that the `LeafletMap` should be conditionally rendered within the `App.tsx` file, but *only* when `currentView === \'user\'` and an `activeBooking` exists. I\'ve updated the homepage to always stay clean, and have incorporated the map in the operator view.\n\n\n**Confirming Map Integration**\n\nI am now confirming the correct map integration. The goal is now clear: embed the `<LeafletMap />` directly within `App.tsx`, with conditional rendering based on both `currentView` and the existence of an `activeBooking`. In the operator view (`currentView === \'operator\'`), the map *must* render. On the homepage (`currentView === \'user\'` with no booking), the map *must* be absent. With an active booking, the map immediately appears in the active trip display.\n\n\n**Verifying Booking Interface**\n\nI\'m now confirming the map integrates into `UserDashboard` for an active booking. I\'ve ensured `LeafletMap` renders within the tracking container once booking is submitted, not on the homepage. I\'m verifying proper cleanup during unmount and re-mount, ensuring a smooth transition to the tracking view. The initial display centers on pickup coordinates, including markers and accuracy circle, as expected.\n\n\n\n**Refining Map Siren Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the ambulance display. I\'m ensuring the active ambulance siren ring and route are *only* visible after operator acceptance, and are not shown during the `\'pending\'` stage. I\'ve also validated that the map centers on the pickup coordinates, and updates when those coordinates change.\n\n\n"
