  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Button Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `HoldToConfirmButton` component, particularly its use of `requestAnimationFrame` and the SVG progress ring. I\'m checking when the `disabled` state changes or when the hold action completes. I see that when `disabled` is true or the hold is completed, something is happening.\n\n\n**Refining Reset Logic**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down on the reset behavior of the booking button. Specifically, I\'m verifying the logic that handles the `disabled` state changes. I\'m satisfied that when the button is re-enabled after being disabled (due to cancellation, completion, etc.), the `isCompleted` flag and progress are correctly reset. This ensures the button is ready for a fresh booking attempt. I\'m focusing on ensuring it\'s a seamless experience.\n\n\n**Analyzing Map Integration**\n\nI\'m currently focused on map integration within the user dashboard. I\'ve noted the conditions under which the active booking live card is displayed, checking the `activeBooking` object and its `stage` property. This lets me understand when the map view is presented in relation to the active booking\'s status. I need to figure out what is shown after the current `But` state.\n\n\n**Mapping the Integration**\n\nOkay, so I just figured something out: the Leaflet map component is no longer nested within the `UserDashboard`, but now lives directly within `App.tsx`! This is key. On the homepage when there is no active booking, no map is shown. As soon as a booking starts (either with a long hold or a manual submit), the UI jumps to an active booking/tracking interface, and the map immediately appears right at the top. The map container shows all the relevant location data as expected. If the booking stage is pending, we have the ambulance search status message.\n\n\n**Visualizing Unit Dispatch**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the unit dispatch visualization on the map. The animation of the radar scanning is something I\'m thinking through. Also, I\'m considering the transition. I am visualizing the operator acceptance button, and how the user can test the transition both manually and automatically. I\'m keen on how this will show the ambulance marker and the road-following route. I also want to make sure the Live telemetry is represented.\n\n\n**Verifying Map Accessibility**\n\nI\'ve confirmed map access. It\'s available to both users in the active booking/tracking interface and operators in their dashboard for fleet management. The map appears in the App.tsx for the user (when active booking), and the OperatorDashboard (when the view is operator). The location accuracy is essential to center the map on the user. I\'ve also verified the map is never visible on the homepage when there is no active booking. I\'m exploring how the addresses display on the map (e.g. pickup & dropoff), plus the document attachment. I need to handle when the operator switches their view.\n\n\n**Confirming Map Centering**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'ve been examining the map centering logic in `LeafletMap.tsx`. I\'ve confirmed it centers on `pickupCoords` upon initialization and updates when `pickupCoords` changes. The code uses `L.map` with `center` set to `pickupCoords` with zoom 14. If a route exists, it fits bounds between ambulance, pickup, and destination. Otherwise, the map centers on the pickup location.\n\n\n**Evaluating GPS Implementation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `handleDetectGPS` function and how it interfaces with user booking. I\'m confirming that accurate GPS coordinates are obtained using `navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition` with high accuracy. I also check how it handles GPS failures and permissions denials gracefully. Moreover, I\'m verifying the logic that ensures the user can proceed either with or without GPS, using either the detected or selected pickup location for the booking.\n\n\n**Tracking Vehicle Location**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now tracing the logic for vehicle position updates during a trip. The initial booking sets `stage` to \'pending\'. This then triggers a series of state changes as the booking progresses, especially the `routePolyline`. The route is initially empty. I will track the state changes during dispatch and vehicle movements, watching for updates.\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Workflow**\n\nI\'m now tracing the logic that follows after the initial booking submission. I\'ve noted the `stage` transitions to \"pending,\" accompanied by the \"Ambulance Search Active\" banner. The user interface presents the pickup marker with a radar pulse, the destination hospital marker, and the status message. I\'m focusing on the states after the \"Accept Emergency Request\" button click. I will track the state changes during dispatch and vehicle movements, watching for updates.\n\n\n**Simulating Operator Acceptance**\n\nI\'m now investigating operator acceptance. I\'m focusing on simulating this scenario with both manual and automatic options. The UI now features both an \"Accept Now\" button and a timed auto-acceptance feature, complete with a countdown pill, which can be overridden, so I can test edge cases. I\'m keen to test the scenarios that play out when either method is used.\n\n\n**Observing Unit Dispatch**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the dispatch process, observing the state changes and map updates closely. Specifically, I see that upon operator acceptance, the ambulance marker, the road route to pickup, and the toast appear as expected. The map then displays the real-time ambulance movement, tracking its position and updating the route polyline dynamically as the vehicle moves to the pickup location.\n\n\n**Reviewing Homepage Transition**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now investigating the transition back to the homepage after a trip completes. I\'ve noted the `activeBooking` object becomes `null`, and the map disappears, per requirements. The active tracking card vanishes, and the user returns to the homepage. I\'m focusing on ensuring the homepage display is clean and focused. Specifically, the manual booking form and \"Hold to confirm\" button, currently below the tracking card, should be re-displayed on the homepage. I need to make sure the user\'s view isn\'t confusing after a completed trip.\n\n\n**Verifying UI Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the transitions between the homepage and the booking interface, particularly the logic triggered after holding the booking button for 5 seconds or submitting a manual booking form. I\'ve noted that the interactive map appears immediately after submission within the booking/tracking interface. Also, I\'ve confirmed that the homepage, with its manual booking form and \"Hold to confirm\" button, is displayed when there is no active booking. The active tracking interface disappears upon trip completion, returning the user to the clean homepage.\n\n\n**Updating Booking Stages**\n\nI\'m now implementing the API changes. I\'ve updated `/api/bookings` to support the \'pending\' stage with an optional `assignedUnitId`, and it returns the booking accordingly. I\'m also addressing `/api/bookings/:id/stage` to handle the assignment of the nearest ambulance and route updates for \'accepted\' or \'en_route_pickup\' stages. I\'ve refactored `App.tsx` and removed the Leaflet map from the main render.\n\n\n\n**Refining Workflow Transitions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the workflow after the ambulance is accepted. The current focus is on the `handleAcceptBooking` function, ensuring it selects the nearest ambulance, calculates the route, and transitions the booking stage to `\'en_route_pickup\'`. I\'m working on how this can be triggered by either an automated timer or a manual button press, to streamline the testing.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now integrating the additional UI elements for different booking stages. I see that during the \'pending\' stage, I have implemented the ambulance search banner with live radar, and the operator simulation. For \'accepted\', \'en route\', and \'transporting\' stages, I am now focused on integrating live telemetry, driver details, the trip stepper, and patient information. I\'m making sure to incorporate the address, and the attachments.\n\n\n**Validating Map Display**\n\nI\'m now confirming the map display behavior when the operator view is active. I see that the `<LeafletMap />` component is included within the `<OperatorDashboard />`, ensuring operators can view fleet locations and active dispatch routes. I\'ve also verified the map centers on the pickup point during the `pending` stage, displaying a radar scan aura. I can see the controls. I also want to make sure the app works without the active telemetry.\n\n\n"
