  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dashboard Structure**\n\nI\'m currently breaking down the components of `UserDashboard.tsx`. I\'m focusing on the props it accepts and the elements it renders. Right now, I\'m specifically looking at the interface for the props, to get a clear sense of its dependencies and functionality.\n\n\n**Refining UI Flow**\n\nI\'m now thinking through the user flow, specifically regarding map display. The primary focus is to remove the map from the home screen for a cleaner interface. The map should be displayed only *after* the user initiates a booking. This aims for a more focused initial experience, streamlining the ambulance booking process.\n\n\n**Defining Map Integration**\n\nI\'ve decided to display the interactive map exclusively after a booking is confirmed, either through a 5-second button hold or manual form submission. The map, centered on the user\'s pickup location, will show pickup and destination markers, the ambulance, and route. Initially, it\'ll display ambulance-search status, then the accepted ambulance and route. I\'m choosing to pass a `mapElement` prop for seamless embedding into the booking interface.\n\n\n**Implementing Booking Status Display**\n\nI\'m now implementing the display of active booking details. When an `activeBooking` prop is present in `UserDashboard`, I\'ll render the Active Booking & Tracking Screen. This will include the status bar with the tracking code and a stage pill, and a series of quick actions that vary based on the booking stage. For instance, the \"Accept Dispatch\" button and auto-acceptance countdown will appear when `stage` is pending, but other stages will have different quick actions.\n\n\n**Designing Booking Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on what a user sees when they request an ambulance and it is assigned to them. I\'m visualizing an interactive map centered on their location, with a pulsing pickup marker and destination marker. If the booking is in a pending state, I\'ll show a dynamic banner with real-time telemetry like ETA and distance. Also, I\'m thinking about including a card with the driver and paramedic details, as well as an interactive stage stepper.\n\n\n**Visualizing Post-Booking State**\n\nI\'m now outlining what the `UserDashboard` displays after a booking is confirmed. I\'m focusing on a multi-stage process: \"En Route to Pickup,\" \"On Scene,\" \"Transporting to ER,\" and \"At Hospital ER\" and \"Transfer Complete\". I\'m planning to create a \"Patient Booking Details & Symptoms Card\". It will include the patient\'s information, requested equipment badges, pickup/drop-off addresses, an upload button via Cloudinary, and an attached documents list. When `activeBooking === null`, it renders the homepage with sub-navigation tabs like \"Emergency Dispatch,\" \"Hospital Directory,\" \"Trip History,\" and \"Patient Medical ID.\"\n\n\n**Clarifying Homepage Display**\n\nI\'m now making sure the homepage has no map elements. The structure is now set: Tab 1 is the Emergency Dispatch. I\'ve broken the Emergency Dispatch down into two parts, a large 5-second hold button to dispatch the ambulance automatically, or a \"Manual Booking\" button to expand a form to configure the booking. I need to make sure the user flow makes sense. The second tab, of course, is the hospital directory.\n\n\n**Confirming Map Visibility**\n\nI\'ve just reinforced that the map is *never* on the user homepage when there\'s no active booking. I\'ve clearly defined that it only appears within the tracking interface when `activeBooking` is present. Additionally, I\'m now verifying that both booking methods consistently lead to the same tracking interface and map display. I\'m also confirming the operator view and its map.\n\n\n**Defining Map Display Logic**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the map display logic, particularly when `activeBooking` exists. I\'ve realized that the `<LeafletMap />` component should be embedded directly within the `<UserDashboard />`\'s tracking view. For operator view, the `<LeafletMap />` will be part of the `<OperatorDashboard />`. I\'m going through the booking process, and I\'m ensuring the pending state is represented accurately.\n\n\n**Simulating Booking Acceptance**\n\nI\'m now integrating an \"Accept Dispatch\" button directly into the status bar, alongside an auto-accept timer. If no operator action occurs within 6 seconds, the dispatch center automatically assigns the nearest ambulance. Both scenarios, whether operator-initiated or auto-assigned, trigger `handleAcceptBooking(activeBooking.id)`. This action identifies the nearest ambulance, calculates the route, updates the ambulance and booking status (including route polyline), and places the assigned ambulance on the map, complete with details and a siren ring, which then moves toward the pickup location.\n\n\n**Animating Ambulance Movement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the vehicle animation within `App.tsx`. I\'ve realized that the animation only activates when an ambulance is en route. It will only begin animating once an ambulance is dispatched and a route is established. Initially, while in the \"pending\" state, the animation doesn\'t run, as `routePolyline` is empty. The animation uses the ambulance\'s route coordinates to move.\n\n\n"
