  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dashboard Logic**\n\nI\'ve been examining lines 181-300 of `UserDashboard.tsx`. I see that when there\'s an `activeBooking` that isn\'t completed or cancelled, the current setup renders a tracking card and then, on line 296, renders `{/* 1. HOMEP`. I need to figure out the intended purpose of this HOMEP render, and understand its relation to the tracking card.\n\n\n**Clarifying Interface States**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the interface states. It\'s clear that the clean homepage is for non-active bookings, displaying booking options and the hospital network. With an active booking, the map is intended to appear after a successful hold or manual form submission. The map, centered on the user\'s location, will display the route and ambulance details. It\'s becoming easier to see how the initial render relates to the active booking interface design.\n\n\n**Defining Active Booking Views**\n\nI\'m now detailing the active booking display. After acceptance, I must show the assigned unit, the route, live telemetry, the vehicle moving on coordinates, the driver card, a direct call button, 3D telemetry, a share link, the cancel request option, the stage stepper, and medical details. I\'ve confirmed the emergency hold button isn\'t needed during an active booking. Once complete or cancelled, the user returns to the homepage. I\'ve also confirmed sub-nav tab switching, and that the Leaflet map will render on the Emergency Dispatch tab when there is an active booking.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Map Data**\n\nI\'m now identifying how the `LeafletMap` gets its data within `UserDashboard.tsx`. I\'ve confirmed it\'s rendered when there\'s an active booking. The props it requires are clear: `ambulances`, `routePolyline`, `routeError`, `onRecalculateRoute`, and `onAcceptBooking`. I\'ve matched `activeBooking` properties to map props: `pickupCoords`, `dropoffCoords`, `activeAmbulanceId`, and `isSearching`. The structure is quite elegant, and I like how modular this is. I\'ve also tracked how the homepage coordinates affect the current booking.\n\n\n**Tracking Route Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now investigating the coordinate flow. I\'ve realized the tracking interface directly uses `activeBooking.pickupCoords` and `activeBooking.dropoffCoords`. If the user drags the pickup marker during the active trip, `onPickupChange` updates `activeBooking.pickupCoords`, directly modifying the active booking data. This ties neatly into Requirement 4, which is about dynamic tracking and the display of the ambulance route and position. I see now how operator acceptance is handled: in `App.tsx`, acceptance triggers an ambulance search and route calculation.\n\n\n**Mapping Route Generation**\n\nI\'m now focused on route generation. I\'ve been dissecting the asynchronous `getDrivingRoute` function call. I see that the primary route is built from the ambulance\'s location and the pickup coordinates. The `try...catch` block gracefully handles route calculation errors, falling back to a direct line between these points. This logic ensures the route updates the `phase1Polyline`, `dispatchDistKm`, and `dispatchEtaMins` states. The code also updates ambulance status and booking states accordingly.\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Lifecycle**\n\nI\'m now examining how an ambulance booking transitions to the en route stage after dispatch. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the state updates within the `handleCreateBooking` function. The code updates booking properties like `stage`, `assignedUnitId`, and route details, like `routePolyline`. It also updates timestamp metadata. The updates to ambulance status and booking states in `App.tsx` seem especially important, and provide clear linkage to the display within `UserDashboard.tsx`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Booking Creation**\n\nI\'m now focused on booking creation. I\'ve been reviewing the logic in the `handleCreateBooking` function. This function creates a new booking object. It sets the initial stage to \'pending\', the `assignedUnitId` to `undefined`, and populates the booking object with patient details, pickup/dropoff information, requested equipment and route details. The booking also receives an automatically generated ID and tracking code. I\'m noting how the initial stage influences the subsequent user interface.\n\n\n**Examining Booking API**\n\nI\'m now examining the API call within `handleCreateBooking`. I can see this uses `fetch` to POST booking details to `/api/bookings`. The request body includes patient and trip details, and the booking stage. It\'s interesting that the API call is asynchronous and doesn\'t block the UI, even though an API sync failure is handled. I need to understand better how the backend processes and handles this request and possible failure states.\n\n\n\n**Observing Initial Booking Stages**\n\nI\'m now tracing the immediate UI changes after booking creation. I\'ve noted that `activeBooking` immediately becomes the new booking, and the tracking interface opens with the interactive Leaflet map. The radar effect on the pickup marker is showing, the destination hospital marker is present, and the booking stage is pending. The status banner confirms the ambulance search is active and that triage is transmitted to dispatch, and the user has a \"Simulate Operator Acceptance\" button.\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Acceptance**\n\nI\'m now tracing the logic when `handleAcceptBooking(activeBooking.id)` is called. The system assigns the nearest ambulance and calculates the driving route to the pickup location. The `routePolyline` is set and drawn, the ambulance marker appears with its siren ring, and the vehicle begins moving. Live ETA, distance, and driver details are shown. As the ambulance nears the patient, the status changes to \'on_scene\'. I am noting the state changes and UI updates throughout this process.\n\n\n"
