  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Structure Progress**\n\nI\'m impressed by the clean structure of `UserDashboard.tsx`. I\'ve begun reviewing the component\'s structure in more depth, and will examine how the props are defined. I will need to update `UserDashboardProps`, and am starting with `currentPickup`.\n\n\n**Refining Prop Definitions**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `UserDashboardProps`, meticulously assessing each entry. Specifically, the `onDropoffChange` function and its handling of the `hospitalId` parameter require careful attention. I want to ensure the logic and type definitions are as clean and concise as possible, potentially consolidating or re-factoring some types for improved readability.\n\n\n**Tracing Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m now tracing the control flow within Tab 1, specifically when activeBooking exists and isn\'t completed or canceled. I\'ve mapped the exact conditions that determine the display of the active booking and live tracking interface, including the titles, subtitles, and badges based on the booking stage. The key is how I render the Top Bar, with title and badge text, which is based on the booking stage, and will vary based on `activeBooking.stage`. I\'ve also identified the user actions, and their effects.\n\n\n**Mapping Visual Elements**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the interactive Leaflet map within the component. I\'m examining how `pickupCoords`, `dropoffCoords`, and `ambulances` are used. I\'m evaluating the `activeAmbulanceId`, and also the `routePolyline` data, and how it is used. I\'m looking at how user interaction via `onPickupChange` and `onDropoffChange` is handled.\n\n\n**Evaluating Telemetry Displays**\n\nI\'m now examining the real-time telemetry strip within Tab 1. I\'ve focused on how different values, like ETA, distance, and dispatched unit, are displayed based on the `activeBooking.stage`. Specifically, I\'m noting how the UI dynamically changes between \"pending\" and other stages, including the handling of `assignedAmbulance`. I\'m verifying the display of driver details and emergency controls, and ensuring that the logic handles both the display and actions correctly.\n\n\n**Designing Stage Display**\n\nI\'m now designing the stage progression stepper with all seven stages and their visual representation. I\'m focusing on how the current, active stage and the completed past stages are rendered. I\'ll make sure it has the ability to advance the active stage for testing, and review the patient details card. It will show pickup and destination addresses with badges and patient details.\n\n**Constructing Homepage Display**\n\nI\'m now rendering the homepage display when there\'s no active booking, or the booking is completed/cancelled. I\'m envisioning a clean, professional emergency dispatch hero section with a gradient accent, a descriptive title, and an action subtitle. I\'m focusing on how to communicate the emergency response clearly.\n\n\n**Formulating UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now starting to structure the initial homepage view for dispatch. I\'m focusing on the main components like the GPS status bar, emergency dispatch button, and manual booking option. I need to handle cases where GPS fails, and then present manual booking with the key selections like pickup, destination, and medical equipment with patient information. I\'m focusing on the user flow for initiating a dispatch.\n\n\n**Verifying Map Logic**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the map\'s correct behavior! The homepage deliberately omits the map. Bookings, initiated via either the hold button or manual entry, activate the tracking interface, complete with a map displaying the location. It accurately centers on pickup, displays pickup and destination markers, the ambulance, and the dynamic road-following route. Both initiation methods work correctly, and duplicate requests are prevented, with GPS and routing errors handled gracefully.\n\n\n"
