  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dashboard Stages**\n\nOkay, I\'m currently cross-referencing stage definitions in `OperatorDashboard.tsx` with those in `UserDashboard.tsx`, specifically focusing on stages 4 and 5: \"on_scene\" and \"transporting,\" respectively. I\'m trying to align these crucial points: \"On Scene / Assessing\" and \"Transporting to ER\" \342\200\223 or the more dynamic \"Patient Picked Up / Transporting to ER.\" I want to ensure consistency across the user interface.\n\n\n**Aligning Status Indicators**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `App.tsx` file. I\'m focusing on how the status indicators and animations automatically update. Specifically, I\'m verifying the connection between the \'transporting\' stage and the \"Patient Picked Up / Transporting to Hospital ER\" status. I\'m cross-referencing this animation logic with the stage definitions in both dashboard components to maintain consistency.\n\n\n**Refining Status Updates**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the transition from the \"on_scene\" stage to \"transporting.\" My focus is the `setAmbulances` update within the `pickup` function and its interactions with `handleAdvanceStage`. I\'m specifically looking at the status update to \"on_scene\" when the ambulance reaches the patient. I must also consider the logic in advancing the stage, and how that influences the mapping of the new route. I\'m making sure to correlate this correctly to the user request.\n\n\n**Implementing Route Refresh**\n\nI\'m now making immediate updates after the \"Patient Picked Up\" event. My priority is to clear the existing route and request a fresh, road-accurate route to the hospital. I\342\200\231ll make sure to update the destination marker, ETA, and navigation instructions. I will synchronize these changes across user and operator interfaces. I am taking care to ensure that outdated routing responses are ignored and obsolete route layers are cleared before showing new routes, preventing race conditions. I will handle routing failures with an error message and retry mechanism. I\'m focusing on fixing any existing stale polylines.\n\n\n**Correcting Route Updates**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a crucial bug: the `handleAdvanceStage` function was not updating the `routePolyline` within the `bookings` state, leading to outdated route information. Specifically, when `nextStage` became \'transporting,\' the function was setting the local `routePolyline` but not updating the `activeBooking.routePolyline`. This caused stale route polylines to persist, preventing proper updates to the user interface and leading to incorrect route displays. I must fix this.\n\n\n**Fixing Asynchronous Routing**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue in `App.tsx`: asynchronous route fetching conflicts with synchronous state updates. The `setBookings` function, within `handleAdvanceStage`, was updating the booking state before the `getDrivingRoute` call completed, resulting in stale routing data. This means that important route details like `routePolyline`, `etaMinutes`, and `distanceKm` were not being updated. Furthermore, the previous route wasn\'t immediately cleared after \"Patient Picked Up.\" I\'m now focusing on reordering these operations to ensure route data consistency and adherence to requirements.\n\n\n**Addressing Routing Consistency**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updates to ensure the route starts from the ambulance\'s latest position, whether it is the current location or pickup coordinates. I\'m focusing on ensuring outdated routes are purged before presenting the new route. I must make sure the server booking is also updated with the new route data. Finally, I\'ll need to reset the `waypointIndexRef` to restart animations when a new route is presented.\n\n```\n```\n\n"
