  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Route Conditions**\n\nI\'m currently focused on conditional logic surrounding route calculations. Specifically, I\'m examining the differences between `calculateCurrentRoute` behavior with and without an active booking. I\'m noting that the homepage doesn\'t display a map when there\'s no active booking. Now, I\'m turning my attention to how `onRecalculateRoute` should operate in the presence of an active booking.\n\n\n**Clarifying Recalculation Logic**\n\nI\'ve clarified the recalculation logic in failure scenarios. When the user initiates a recalculation, the new route depends on the `activeBooking` stage. If routing fails or the user clicks \"Recalculate\" during the `transporting` stage, the route is recalculated from the ambulance\'s current location to the hospital. If the booking is in `en_route_pickup`, it recalculates the ambulance-to-pickup route. Without a booking, it recalculates pickup-to-dropoff. I\'m also now reviewing the animation loop, particularly how `waypointIndexRef` resets when routes change.\n\n\n**Examining Hospital Route Updates**\n\nI\'m now diving into the behavior when an ambulance is transporting. If `routePolyline` is empty, the animation stops. When a new hospital route is set, `waypointIndexRef` resets to zero, and the ambulance progresses from the pickup location to the hospital. Reaching the hospital updates the ambulance status and stops movement. I\'m also now going to examine what happens if the operator directly advances the stage.\n\n\n**Initiating Hospital Route Transition**\n\nMy focus is now on the transition to the hospital route after the operator advances the stage. I\'ve initiated clearing the old route by resetting `routePolyline` and `waypointIndexRef`. Simultaneously, I\'m updating `bookings` to reflect a `transporting` stage with an empty `routePolyline`, and setting ambulance status to `transporting`. Now, I\'m starting an asynchronous route calculation to the hospital, using either the ambulance\'s current location or pickup coordinates as the origin.\n\n\n**Implementing Hospital Route Update**\n\nI\'m now implementing the update to the hospital route. I\'ve integrated a check to prevent race conditions during routing requests, discarding older requests if a newer one is triggered. I\'m now setting the new route polyline, distance, ETA, and resetting `waypointIndexRef`. The bookings state is being updated to reflect the new route details, including the ambulance\'s current position. I\'m also syncing this information to the server.\n\n\n**Addressing Route Failure Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the system reacts to route failures during the transport stage. I\'ve considered that if the hospital route calculation fails, I\'ve cleared the previous route. The system now displays an error message with a retry option. I\'ve also incorporated a handler for this retry, which I\'ll test. This includes logic for handling route failures during different stages, such as `transporting` or `en_route_pickup`.\n\n\n**Refactoring Route Fetch Logic**\n\nI\'m now refactoring the route fetching logic into dedicated helpers. I\'ve extracted the logic to fetch the pickup route based on an active booking and the assigned ambulance. Now, I\'m defining a dedicated `fetchHospitalRoute` function. I\'m focusing on incorporating an early route clearance and a cancellation mechanism to prevent stale requests.\n\n\n**Finalizing Hospital Route Handling**\n\nI\'m now finalizing the hospital route handling. I am setting the new route polyline, distance, ETA, and resetting waypoint index. I\'m updating the bookings state to reflect these new route details, including the ambulance\'s current position, and syncing this information to the server. I have incorporated an error handler for the server call and will implement a proper retry mechanism in the next iteration.\n\n\n**Analyzing Stage Transitions**\n\nI\'m now examining how stage transitions affect route updates and ambulance status. I\'ve noted that setting `setRoutePolyline([])` signifies a completed route, which resets the ambulance\'s speed and position. Arriving on scene sets the location to pickup coordinates and updates the booking stage to `on_scene`. Reaching the hospital updates the location to dropoff coordinates and stage to `at_hospital`. Returning to base uses the dropoff coordinates to assigned unit base location.\n\n\n**Updating Route Rendering**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `tripStage` prop into the map rendering. I\'ve updated the `LeafletMap.tsx` component to handle `tripStage` changes, specifically focusing on the conditional rendering of the `routePolyline`. Now, I\'m defining a distinct color, like `#0ea5e9` with bold amber/white dashed center or glowing red-blue emergency route style (`#dc2626` or `#0284c7`), for the hospital transport route when `tripStage` is `\'transporting\'`. Finally, I\'m ensuring the map fits bounds to the new route.\n\n\n**Refining Marker Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the marker display logic based on `tripStage`. For pickup, I\'m implementing different states for `transporting`, `at_hospital`, and `completed`. I\'m defining a badge and dimmed opacity for the pickup marker when complete and adding `SCANNING PICKUP` with a radar pulse when searching. For the dropoff marker, I\'m setting an active emergency destination beacon for the `transporting` stage with a prominent badge. I\'ve also incorporated the `routeError` display.\n\n\n**Adjusting Display Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the display of route information based on the `tripStage` prop. I\'m updating how the `routePolyline` is rendered, ensuring the correct one is always shown. I\'m modifying the telemetry strip to clearly display ETAs and distances specific to the `transporting` stage, clarifying ETA to Hospital ER information. I\'m making sure it displays either the hospital ETA or the live ETA based on the current stage and distance traveled.\n\n\n**Implementing Stage-Based Updates**\n\nI\'m now integrating stage-specific UI updates for smoother operator interaction. I\'ve added quick action buttons for \"Patient Picked Up\" and \"Arrived at Hospital ER\" based on the `activeBooking.stage`. I\'m also implementing the informational banner and error handling for route errors. My current focus is on the banner and button logic.\n\n\n**Refining Transport UI**\n\nI\'m now refining the transport UI within the operator dashboard to improve clarity. I\'m focusing on dynamic updates to maneuvers based on the `transporting` stage. I\'ve updated the next maneuver cue to read \"HOSPITAL EMERGENCY BAY ENTRY\" and added the dropoff address for more specificity. Now, I\'m adjusting the speed mode display to \"48 KM/H (Patient On Board)\" and ensuring that the ETA matches `selectedBooking.etaMinutes`.\n\n\n**Finalizing Operator Interface**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the operator interface, specifically addressing button labels and maneuver cues. I\'m ensuring the stage advancement buttons use \"Patient Picked Up (Transport to ER)\" for `transporting` and \"Advance: Patient Picked Up (Route to Hospital)\" for `on_scene`. The next maneuver cue displays the appropriate phrases for different stages. When `selectedBooking.stage === \'on_scene\'`, it reads \"ON SCENE \342\200\224 PATIENT TRIAGE\" and includes specific pickup address. \"ARRIVED AT ER\" is displayed in the other stage and describes patient transfer. Finally, I\'m integrating these changes with the server update logic.\n\n\n**Integrating Server Updates**\n\nI\'m now integrating the server updates for route details. I\'ve located the `/api/bookings/:id/stage` endpoint in `server.ts` and can readily update it to store the `routePolyline`, `distanceKm`, and `etaMinutes` that\'s changed in `routePolyline`. Now, I will look at the map for `pickupMarkerRef` and `dropoffMarkerRef`.\n\n\n"
