  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Isolating User Controls**\n\nI\'m currently focused on separating user controls from operator functions. The goal is to remove the operator-specific tools like time skips, simulation buttons, and manual status controls from the User Panel. This helps in enhancing user accessibility. I\'ll retain these controls within the Operator Panel and Demo Controls.\n\n\n**Refining Panel Access**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on restricting user access to strictly customer-facing features. My focus is on the `UserDashboard.tsx` file, carefully examining operator-specific buttons and controls like \"Accept Dispatch\" and \"Patient Picked Up.\" I\'m also ensuring the removal of time skips and manual status changes. \"Reset Demo\" needs to be in the operator section or demo controls. My next step will be to implement the locking of locations after booking.\n\n\n**Implementing Location Locking**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing location locking after booking submission. My next goal is to prevent users from altering pickup and destination locations once a booking is active. I\'m focusing on the `UserDashboard.tsx` and `LeafletMap.tsx` files. I am working to ensure markers are not draggable when `isLocked`. I also want to disable location changes in the User Panel. I will display the confirmed locations as read-only.\n\n\n**Enforcing Cancellation Limits**\n\nI\'m now implementing restrictions on booking cancellations based on trip stage. I\'m focusing on the `App.tsx` and `server.ts` files. Specifically, I am writing logic in `handleCancelActiveBooking` to disallow cancellations once a booking reaches the `transporting`, `at_hospital`, or `completed` stages. A clear error message will be displayed in the User Panel. The server-side cancellation endpoint must also enforce these rules.\n\n\n"
