  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Environment Variables**\n\nI\'m currently looking into the environment variable configuration. It seems there\'s only a `.env.example` file present, not a `.env` file. I\'m checking `server.ts` to see how the code is handling this, particularly how it\'s accessing variables like `CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME` and `CLOUDINARY_API_KEY`, and if default values are being used.\n\n\n**Analyzing Cloudinary Upload Methods**\n\nI\'ve outlined two primary Cloudinary upload pathways: backend-signed uploads (requiring API key and secret) and unsigned uploads (using a preset). I\'m focusing on handling situations where credentials might be missing or invalid. I\'m planning to ensure the application remains functional by employing a fallback mechanism that will return a high-fidelity local persistent data URL with a public ID, while also clearly communicating any issues to the user.\n\n\n**Evaluating Cloudinary & Ride Sim**\n\nI\'ve crafted an `api/cloudinary/config` endpoint that exposes whether Cloudinary is configured (based on presence of cloud name and either API key or upload preset), along with the cloud name, but crucially, without exposing secrets. I\'m also preparing for tests of uploads and retrievals. Shifting gears, I\'m now fully immersed in the ride simulation requirements, planning how to generate 20 rides per simulated day with randomized parameters and realistic delays and storage.\n\n\n**Defining Simulation Engine Architecture**\n\nI\'m now diving into the design of the background simulation engine. I\'m focusing on defining what constitutes a \"simulated day\" in the context of an interactive web application. I need to make the simulation user-friendly, not requiring users to wait for real-time hours, such as a 24-hour cycle to see twenty rides. I\'m considering options for a simulated clock, speed multipliers, and a batch schedule. I\'m also thinking of how to implement start, pause, and reset controls, along with tracking its status.\n\n\n**Constructing Ride Simulation Attributes**\n\nI\'m now deep in the ride simulation data model. I\'ve defined the structure of each simulated ride with properties like a simulation badge, realistic patient names from Delhi NCR, and random pickup locations. I\'m also including destination hospitals from verified locations, and am assigning available ambulances with driver and paramedic details. Realistic timestamps, based on simulation events, and a status history with transitions will be stored, to allow a functional simulation of an actual patient transport.\n\n\n**Modeling Ride Lifecycle Dynamics**\n\nMy focus is the ride lifecycle. I\'m now implementing the ride progression: `assigned` to `en_route_pickup`, then `on_scene`, `transporting`, `at_hospital`, and finally `completed`. Each simulated day will generate approximately twenty rides. The background engine will smoothly advance these rides through the lifecycle with realistic delays. Completed rides free the assigned ambulance for further rides. I\'m ensuring persistence with `serverBookings` on the server and `localStorage` on the client. I\'ve built in Start, Pause, and Reset controls, and will label the generated records appropriately.\n\n\n"
