  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Interface Needs**\n\nI\'m currently focused on Requirement 1: separating operator and user interfaces. I\'m fleshing out the operator dashboard, imagining a comprehensive view with a fleet map, incoming requests, active trips, driver availability, and historical trip data. Now I\'ll need to outline the aspects to remove from the User UI.\n\n\n**Refining Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the automated dispatch of ambulances based on proximity and availability. The operator will monitor and override these assignments. I\'m ensuring we separate user and operator permissions. The operator dashboard should have a dedicated comprehensive layout or tab system featuring a full fleet map, incoming requests, and active trips. I\'m focusing on the integration of the LeafletMap component into the Operator Dashboard.\n\n\n**Detailing Dashboard Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Active Trips\" section of the operator dashboard. It will showcase all current dispatches, including details on ambulance and driver, along with live ETA and patient info, featuring an \"Override Assignment\" dropdown. I\'ve also detailed the \"Driver & Fleet Availability\" section, listing ambulance specifics and driver statuses. Finally, \"Trip History\" will display past dispatches, filterable by real versus simulated, and I\'ll include simulation controls.\n\n\n**Verifying Auto-Dispatch Flow**\n\nNow, I\'m verifying the automated ambulance dispatch. The core concept: Upon a new booking, the system swiftly locates the closest available ambulance. If found, it automatically assigns the unit, updates the booking state to `en_route_pickup`, and promptly notifies both the user and the operator. Pending requests are held and auto-dispatched the instant a unit becomes free. The operator gets full visibility, with the option to override assignments.\n\n\n**Adding Override Control**\n\nMy immediate focus is on refining the operator\'s control over ambulance assignments. I\'m adding an \"Override Assignment\" button or modal to allow for selecting a different unit, especially if a specialized one is needed. Furthermore, I\'ve confirmed that manual approval of every booking is *not* required.\n\nI\'m now switching gears to Requirement 2: automating the daily ride simulation. I will build a background simulation to generate about 20 realistic rides per simulated day, with randomized elements, persistent storage of ride data, and display the information on the operator dashboard. I need start, pause, and reset controls for the simulation. I will be sure to prevent duplicate rides.\n\n\n**Designing Simulation Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now detailing the automated daily ride simulation with state management in `server.ts` and `App.tsx`. I\'ve defined a `simulationState` with a status, day count, time, and ride tallies. I\'ll use 25 simulated patient profiles featuring Delhi NCR landmarks and verified hospital destinations. Simulated ride progression will utilize the pre-defined hospital destinations and available ambulances. Generated records will be clearly labelled as simulated data to avoid confusion or refresh issues.\n\n\n**Modeling Ride Progression**\n\nI\'m now detailing simulated ride progression within the automatic daily simulation, starting from a pending state at a simulated time. The process includes auto-dispatching the nearest available ambulance to \'en_route_pickup\'. The ambulance will then move to the patient, go on scene, and transport the patient to the hospital, eventually returning to available, all in the simulated mode. I\'m adding simulation controls to the operator dashboard, including play/pause/reset buttons, and a speed selector for the simulation. Lastly, I\'m ensuring that simulated rides are labeled as such, and will add a tracking code.\n\n\n**Finalizing Simulation Details**\n\nI\'m now implementing detailed ride simulation features. I\'m adding comprehensive timestamps and status history for each simulated ride, including notes at each stage for clarity. The simulated ride data will be saved to both the server and local storage. On the operator dashboard, I will add clear violet badges to all simulated rides. Finally, I will integrate filter tabs within the operator dashboard to isolate the simulated rides from real-world data.\n\n```text\nI\'m now implementing detailed ride simulation features. I\'m adding comprehensive timestamps and status history for each simulated ride, including notes at each stage for clarity. The simulated ride data will be saved to both the server and local storage. On the operator dashboard, I will add clear violet badges to all simulated rides. Finally, I will integrate filter tabs within the operator dashboard to isolate the simulated rides from real-world data.\n```\n\n**Adding Hospital Access**\n\nI\'m now integrating the \"Hospitals\" functionality. I\'ve confirmed the need for filter options: \"All\", \"Real Bookings Only\", and \"Simulated Only\". I\'ll add a clarification banner about the daily fleet simulation. I\'m focusing on Requirement 3: hospital directory accessibility. I will remove the specific hospital section and move it to a dedicated navbar item, adding the Hospitals button in `Header.tsx` Zone 2, and ensuring the display changes. The button will trigger a dedicated view for hospital listings.\n\n\n**Verifying Hospital Interface**\n\nI\'ve just finished integrating a full hospital directory. The hospitals functionality, including filter options for real and simulated bookings, is implemented and accessible via a dedicated navbar item, satisfying Requirement 3. Specifically, the hospital section is removed from the user homepage and accessible via \"Hospitals\" in the navbar, providing a cleaner booking interface. Now I am transitioning to Requirement 4: implementing Cloudinary integration for image management.\n\n\n**Implementing Cloudinary Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating Cloudinary for image management, which involves several key steps. I\'ve defined the need for endpoints to manage hospital, driver, and ambulance photos, and will handle backend upload signing. I need to understand how Cloudinary\'s backend signing works, and how to properly sort parameters and create SHA-1 hashes for secure uploads. The plan is to send POST requests with the generated signature to Cloudinary\'s API.\n\n\n**Refining Cloudinary Uploads**\n\nI\'m currently focused on error handling for Cloudinary uploads, and have built clear error responses, ensuring that failures return `{ success: false, error: err.message }`. I\'m also planning to implement Cloudinary optimized delivery URL transformations, using parameters like `f_auto,q_auto,w_800,c_fill`. Next, I will add an endpoint and API to destroy images. Now, I\'m working on adding UI upload buttons to the OperatorDashboard for ambulance and driver units, and to HospitalDirectory for hospital images, each triggering a Cloudinary Upload Modal.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_577671"
