    thinking: "**Analyzing Automatic Dispatch**\n\nI\'ve been examining lines 248-266 in `UserDashboard.tsx` to understand the current ambulance search functionality, specifically the \"Awaiting Operator Dispatch\" status. Requirement 4 demands removing manual operator involvement. I\'m now focusing on integrating automatic dispatch based on user bookings. My goal is to streamline the simulation initiation process.\n\n\n**Refining Auto-Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the auto-dispatch process, specifically the 5-second assignment window. The aim is for immediate matching if an ambulance is available, but the system needs to handle scenarios where no ambulances are available or assignment fails gracefully. I am looking into how to display clear status messages and retry options. I\'m investigating how the existing booking creation (`App.tsx` and `server.ts`) handles this currently, especially the countdown or radar search visuals. I\'m prioritizing error handling and user feedback.\n\n\n**Tracking Ambulance Movement**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the issue in `LeafletMap.tsx`, specifically lines 370-478, regarding ambulance marker rendering. I see how the markers are positioned using latitude and longitude coordinates, and I\'m aware that an active booking\'s vehicle position could be tracked. However, I\'ve realized in `App.tsx` that the `amb.location` isn\'t updated along the route polyline during the trip, which explains the ambulance\'s static behavior. The reported user issue highlights that these positions jump between steps.\n\n\n**Developing Movement Engine**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing a movement engine. My goal is to animate the ambulance marker along the route\'s coordinates for a more realistic display. I plan to create a dedicated hook or manager in `src/services/movementEngine.ts` to manage this. I\'m considering running the movement client-side and syncing the state to the server via `/api/sync` to maintain synchronization across dashboards. I need to make sure the animations handle route recalculations after pickup, and stop when a trip ends or is cancelled.\n\n\n**Implementing Route Following**\n\nI\'m now focused on vehicle movement along the `routePolyline`. My plan involves tracking both the `waypointIndex` and interpolation values between waypoints for smooth, realistic movement. I intend to advance the vehicle along the polyline, calculating the bearing from the current point to the next, with an aim to update position every 800-1000ms. I\'m aiming for smooth transitions between coordinates, similar to real-world road trips.\n\n\n**Calculating ETA and Distance**\n\nI\'m now integrating bearing calculations with distance and ETA estimations for the ambulance movement. I\'m focusing on incorporating the `calculateBearing` function, which I found, into my movement engine. I\'m going to sum the distances from the ambulance\'s current location through remaining waypoints. Then, I plan to estimate the ETA based on the remaining distance.\n\n\n**Simulating Stage Advancements**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on simulating stage advancements with the ambulance movement. I\'m updating the booking state with `currentVehiclePosition`, `distanceKm`, and `etaMinutes`. Upon reaching the end of the `routePolyline`, based on the current stage, I\'ll trigger `handleAdvanceStage`. If \'en_route_pickup\', it will automatically trigger \'on_scene\', wait, then \'transporting\', fetching a new route to the hospital. If \'transporting\', it will trigger \'at_hospital\'.\n\n\n**Finalizing Trip Completion**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final stage, transitioning to \'completed\' after a brief delay, and then marking the ambulance as available at its base. I\'m focusing on ensuring the ambulance movement doesn\'t restart on component re-renders or navigation. To avoid this, I plan to store `currentWaypointIndex` and `currentVehiclePosition` within the `booking` object. A single `useEffect` manages the interval, ensuring no duplicate timers and syncing between dashboards via `/api/sync`.\n\n\n**Verifying Requirement Compliance**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the previous phase completely satisfies Requirement 3, ensuring realistic, synchronized ambulance movement with dynamic updates. Now I am shifting my focus to Requirement 1: User Profile Management. I am checking the specifics for a user profile page. I am prioritizing building a \'My Profile\' page to be accessible from the user\'s navigation and/or header.\n\n\n**Designing User Profile Page**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user profile page design, starting with data validation for image uploads, including file size limits, accepted MIME types, and comprehensive error handling. I\'m focusing on features such as replacing or removing profile photos, alongside core personal information fields, emergency contact details, and medical telemetry sections, with input validation and clear visual feedback. The goal is to provide a complete profile page with a \"Save Changes\" and \"Discard Changes\" feature, with data persistence handled via `GET /api/profile` and `POST /api/profile`.\n\n\n**Removing Simulation Card**\n\nI\'m now focused on requirement 2, specifically removing the automated daily ride simulation card from the operator dashboard. I\'ll completely eliminate the simulation\'s visual elements, including the header banner in `OperatorDashboard.tsx` from lines 132-251. Replacing this, I\'ll integrate an operator telemetry and fleet control status bar containing key operational metrics, like active trip counts and fleet availability, plus an incoming emergency queue counter, and Automatic Dispatch Status.\n\n\n**Refining Booking Submission Process**\n\nI\'m now integrating automated booking and ambulance matching. When a user books, I\'ll check for available ambulances within 5 seconds using `ambulances.filter(a => a.status === \'available\')`. If an ambulance is available, I will assign the booking. If not, I\'ll need to handle the no-ambulance scenario by providing a clear status message and retry options for the user. I need to make sure the dashboards update immediately.\n\n\n**Handling No-Ambulance Scenario**\n\nI\'m now focused on the \"no ambulance available\" scenario during booking, ensuring a clear user experience. When no ambulances are available, the booking is initially set to \'pending\' with `noUnitsAvailable: true`. I am displaying a warning message on the user dashboard, indicating all units are deployed, along with a \"Retry Ambulance Search\" button. The objective is to never falsely confirm assignment. When an ambulance is available, I will immediately run a radar dispatch animation in the UI.\n\n\n**Integrating Cloudinary Uploads**\n\nI\'m now integrating Cloudinary for profile photo uploads. I\'m leveraging the existing `/api/cloudinary/upload` endpoint in `server.ts` to validate image formats and sizes. I am aiming to enable seamless uploads in `UserProfileView.tsx`, including file previews and saving the image\'s secure URL and public ID. I will extend the `View` interface in `src/types/index.ts` to support the `profilePhoto` and `profilePhotoPublicId` fields, and add this data to the Cloudinary form.\n\n\n**Implementing Profile Persistence**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the server-side persistence of user profile data. I\'m adding `GET /api/profile` to fetch the profile and `POST /api/profile` to update it, ensuring synchronization. The `/api/sync` endpoint now includes the profile data. I\'m updating `PatientProfile` and related types with email, profile photo, and public ID fields. I\'m using mock data for the initial profile, with a high-resolution photo. I\'m making sure to update the server and data to have this profile.\n\n\n**Adding Profile Detail Fields**\n\nI\'m now integrating fields for Blood Group, Allergies, Medical Conditions, Insurance Provider, and Policy Number into the user profile. I\'ll add a \'Save Changes\' button, persisting to `/api/profile` and `localStorage`, alongside a \'Discard Changes\' button to revert. Success feedback will be displayed via a toast message. I am focused on proper data persistence.\n\n\n"
