  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dynamic Components**\n\nI\'m currently focused on a React component, specifically `UserDashboard.tsx`. I\'ve been examining a dynamic status banner that appears during an ambulance search. I\'m trying to determine the logic for its conditional rendering, which seems to depend on the `activeBooking.stage` and the availability of ambulances based on their `status`. I\'m starting to understand how this component controls UI elements at the state and logic level.\n\n\n**Reviewing Conditional Rendering**\n\nI\'ve been looking deeper into the conditional rendering within the `UserDashboard.tsx` component. The critical section involves the status banner tied to ambulance availability. The current `if` condition checks for `hasAvailable` and `!activeBooking.assignedUnitId`. I\'m focusing on the UI/UX impact when no ambulances are available, noting the alert and retry button. I am trying to determine what logic drives that state.\n\n\n**Investigating Button Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the \"Retry Ambulance Dispatch\" button\'s functionality within the `UserDashboard.tsx` component. The key lies in the conditional rendering of the button itself. I\'m focusing on the `hasAvailable` and `!activeBooking.assignedUnitId` conditions. The goal is to fully grasp the precise circumstances that trigger its display.\n\n\n**Examining Countdown Logic**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the countdown timer\'s behavior within the ambulance dispatch process in `UserDashboard.tsx`. I\'m focused on understanding how the `dispatchCountdown` state variable affects the UI display. Specifically, I\'m trying to determine how it updates and the implications of it being null or undefined. The \"Auto-Matching\" text changes based on `dispatchCountdown`.\n\n\n"
