  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing App Dependencies**\n\nI\'m currently focused on `App.tsx`. I\'ve identified that `calculateBearing` and `calculateHaversineDistance` are imported from `./services/routingService.ts`, while `UserProfileView` comes from `./components/Profile/UserProfileView.tsx`. Now, I\'m checking how `patientProfile` is loaded and saved within this context.\n\n\n**Examining Profile Loading**\n\nI\'ve moved on to examining how `patientProfile` is managed. I see a `useState` hook with a local storage fallback. There\'s a `useEffect` hook that tries to fetch the profile from `/api/profile` on mount, updating the state and local storage.  Finally, I see `handleSaveProfile`, which updates both state and local storage, then attempts a POST request to the same API endpoint.\n\n\n**Investigating Offline Mode**\n\nI\'m now examining the `handleSaveProfile` function to verify the error handling. I see the function attempts a `POST` request, and shows a success toast if `res.ok`. The `try...catch` block gracefully handles server errors, and provides an offline fallback by displaying an \"info\" toast, confirming the save is local. This implies an offline mode capability, which is ideal!\n\n**Examining Ambulance Availability**\n\nI\'m now looking at the booking submission process, specifically the `handleCreateBooking` function within the `App.tsx` file. I\'ve spotted a crucial check for ambulance availability using a filter on the `ambulances` state. If no units are available, the booking is created with a `\'pending\'` status, and a warning is shown. There is a \"Retry Ambulance Dispatch\" button, ready to retry.\n\n\n**Evaluating Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the autonomous dispatch process. If ambulances are available, I see a 5-second countdown is initiated. Around the 3-second mark, a re-evaluation occurs, selecting the nearest ambulance. Then, a route is computed using `routePolyline`, and booking/ambulance statuses are updated to `\'en_route_pickup\'`. The countdown is cleared, and an informative toast is displayed.\n\n\n**Tracking Booking Movement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how bookings are moved along their routes. I\'ve found a `useEffect` hook that sets an interval. This interval clears any existing movement intervals, ensuring no overlap. Inside, it filters for active bookings in specific stages that have route data. It updates booking positions by advancing through route waypoints. I note the use of `movementIntervalRef.current` to manage and clear the interval.\n\n\n**Updating Route Navigation**\n\nI\'m now examining the waypoint progression logic within the movement interval. I see the code calculates bearing using `calculateBearing` and distance with `calculateHaversineDistance`.  It then determines `remainingDistKm`, calculates estimated time of arrival, `remainingEta`, and updates the ambulance state accordingly.\n\n\n**Assessing Stage Advancements**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the stage advancement logic. When an ambulance reaches the end of a route leg, the code checks the booking stage. If it\'s `\'en_route_pickup\'`, a short delay triggers the `\'on_scene\'` stage, followed by an automatic transition to `\'transporting\'`. If the stage is `\'transporting\'`, reaching the end indicates arrival at the hospital, triggering a transition to the `\'at_hospital\'` stage.\n\n\n**Verifying Booking Completion**\n\nI\'m now examining the completion logic within the stage advancement process. I\'ve pinpointed the `useEffect` hook with the interval that handles waypoint progression.  It advances bookings through stages: when the ambulance completes a route, after a short 4-second delay, it will move from \'en_route_pickup\' to the final \'completed\' state, which is shown on both dashboards, after the interval completes. The 850ms interval ensures smooth, continuous updates of the vehicle\'s position, heading, distance, and ETA.\n\n\n**Confirming Cancellation Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how cancelled bookings are handled. When `handleAdvanceStage` updates a booking\'s stage to \'cancelled\', the booking is then excluded from the movement loop, which immediately stops vehicle movement. This verifies the cancellation mechanism. I also note that `movementIntervalRef` is a single timer, addressing potential issues. Lastly, `b.currentWaypointIndex` is preserved to handle component re-renders.\n\n\n"
