    thinking: "**Analyzing Booking Functionality**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `handleCreateBooking` function within `App.tsx`. My current focus is on understanding the differing behaviors when an ambulance is available versus when one isn\'t. I\'m also examining the automatic dispatch requirement and how it\'s handled within this function, referencing the documentation provided.\n\n\n**Refining Automated Dispatch**\n\nMy thoughts now center on the automated dispatch process. I\'m focusing on ensuring immediate ambulance assignment (within 5 seconds!) upon booking submission, or a clear failure message if no ambulances are free. The error handling is critical, so a pending stage is used and the user is provided with a retry option if an ambulance is not available for assignment.\n\n\n**Implementing Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the automated dispatch flow, specifically the 5-second countdown timer. Within that timeframe, I am designing logic to find the nearest available ambulance, calculate a route, and update both the ambulance and the booking with the route details and ETA. I\'m also preparing to display a success toast upon successful dispatch. I have the ambulance movement engine set up, ready to begin iterating.\n\n\n**Simulating Ambulance Movement**\n\nMy focus is now on simulating ambulance movement along a route. I am working through the logic for when an ambulance is en route to pick up a patient or transporting. The `routePolyline` is used, and I\'m incrementing along it, calculating bearing, remaining distance, and ETA. The ambulance\'s location, heading, and speed are being updated. I\'m also ensuring the booking is updated with the current vehicle position, distance, and ETA. I am designing automated state transitions for pickup and arrival. Specifically I\'m preparing to transition to \"on_scene\" and then \"transporting\" at the correct waypoints.\n\n\n"
