  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Component Interactions**\n\nI\'m currently focused on `App.tsx` and the inclusion of `movementIntervalRef` and `dispatchCountdownRef` along with their corresponding state variables. This suggests the intention to manage some form of timer-based actions within the component, specifically related to movement and dispatch logic. The goal appears to be more efficient control of the various processes involved.\n\n\n**Examining Profile Management**\n\nI\'ve moved on to examining the `patientProfile` state variable and its interplay with `localStorage` and a `/api/profile` endpoint. I\'m focusing on the initialization strategy, which attempts to load from `localStorage` first, falling back to a default value if unavailable. There\'s also a `useEffect` hook that fetches the profile from the server on component mount, updating the state and `localStorage` with fetched data. The `handleSaveProfile` function persists changes back to `localStorage`.\n\n\n**Investigating Booking Workflow**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `handleCreateBooking` function. The code checks for an existing active booking before proceeding. It appears the `equipmentIds`, `notes`, and `bookingType` parameters are utilized to construct new bookings. There\'s also a crucial `isSubmittingRef` check to prevent concurrent submissions. The booking type can be either \'emergency_hold\' or \'manual\'.\n\n\n**Assessing Booking Creation**\n\nI\'m now examining the logic within `handleCreateBooking`. I see the code generates a unique booking ID and tracking code. It checks ambulance availability; if none are available, it creates a pending booking. This booking includes patient and location details. The notes field incorporates the `bookingType`, differentiating between \'emergency_hold\' and \'manual\' booking types.\n\n\n**Evaluating Booking Contingency**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the fallback strategy when no ambulances are immediately available for a booking. The code creates a \'pending\' booking, assigning an `undefined` unit and setting ETA and distance to zero. The `stageTimestamps` include only a \'requested\' timestamp. A toast message alerts the user to the deployment status of the emergency fleet. This section also explores the data structure for storing this pending booking. The next step is a focus on booking creation with ambulance availability.\n\n\n**Refining Pending Booking**\n\nI\'m now refining the data structure for a pending booking. It looks like the patient notes incorporate the booking type \342\200\223 either a 5-second emergency hold or a manually assisted note. Key details like pickup and dropoff addresses, equipment, and destination hospital ID are all stored within the booking.  I\'ve noted the booking is initially assigned a \'pending\' stage with an undefined unit and zero ETA and distance. The status history also shows an initial \"pending\" stage. I\'m focusing on how the countdown timer provides user feedback.\n\n\n**Dissecting Dispatch Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `dispatchCountdownRef` and its role within the `handleCreateBooking` process, especially in situations where ambulances are initially unavailable. The countdown decrements with each interval until it reaches a low value where the autonomous dispatch is initiated. I\'m focusing on how the `executeAutonomousDispatch` function is invoked after the countdown expires, and what variables are being passed to this function. It appears that the function handles the logic for assigning an ambulance using the `findNearestAvailableAmbulance` function.\n\n\n**Calculating Route Data**\n\nI\'m now examining how route data is being calculated and utilized within the booking process, specifically focusing on the `getRoute` call. I see the code computes a route using pickup and dropoff coordinates and then stores the resulting polyline, distance, and ETA within the booking\'s state. These values are then applied to update the booking stage to `en_route_pickup` and update related metadata. It appears that the `currentVehiclePosition` is also updated.\n\n\n**Analyzing Dispatch Outcomes**\n\nI\'m now examining how an ambulance assignment that occurs after the autonomous dispatch is handled, as indicated by the stage being updated to \'en_route_pickup\'. It seems the code updates the booking\'s history with a descriptive note about the auto-dispatch, including call sign and driver. There\'s also a toast message to provide feedback to the user and a post to /api/bookings with route data. I also see that the assigned ambulance\'s status will be updated as well.\n\n\n**Tracing Ambulance Assignment**\n\nI\'m now tracing the ambulance assignment logic, focusing on the autonomous dispatch process. Specifically, I\'m examining how the `executeAutonomousDispatch` function gets called and what variables are passed to this function. It appears that it not only handles immediate assignment, but also implements a retry mechanism, triggered by the `handleRetryDispatch` function, in case of initial unavailability. The code updates the ambulance\'s status to \'en_route_pickup\' and includes the current booking ID.\n\n\n**Tracing Booking Movement**\n\nI\'m now tracing the ambulance movement engine, starting from the \'en_route_pickup\' stage. The code iterates through active bookings every 850ms, updating their positions based on the `routePolyline`. It checks if the current stage is `en_route_pickup` or `transporting`, and the availability of `routePolyline` and `assignedUnitId`. I see it updates the `currentWaypointIndex` and calculates the ambulance\'s position.\n\n\n**Evaluating Ambulance Movement**\n\nNow, I\'m analyzing the ambulance position updates within the \'en_route_pickup\' or \'transporting\' stage, specifically how the `routePolyline` is used. The code calculates the ambulance\'s bearing and ETA based on the polyline. The remaining distance along the route is computed, and the ambulance\'s state, including location, heading, and speed, is updated.\n\n\n**Examining Leg Transitions**\n\nI\'m now examining how the ambulance transitions between different legs within a booking. I\'ve noted that the movement logic checks if `nextIndex` has reached the end of the current `routePolyline`. If the ambulance is at the end of `en_route_pickup`, it transitions to `on_scene` with a short delay, then automatically to `transporting` after 4 seconds. Similarly, transitioning to `transporting` leads to `at_hospital`, followed by `completed`.\n\n\n**Reviewing Transportation Transitions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the effects of the stage transitions, particularly from \"on_scene\" to \"transporting\". I\'ve observed updates to the ambulance\'s state, including a change in status and an assigned speed within `handleAdvanceStage`. The code then computes a new route, incorporating the new drop-off location and calling `getRoute` to fetch the polyline, distance, and ETA.\n\n\n**Investigating Route Calculation**\n\nI\'m now examining the route calculation when transitioning to the \'transporting\' stage, specifically how the route from the origin to the hospital is determined and applied to the booking. The code fetches the new route using `getDrivingRoute`, updates state variables like the polyline, distance, and ETA, and then updates the specific booking with this new route information. This also includes an update of the vehicle\'s position.\n\n\n"
